tricted): R / [(π/N)^3 S_HL_full(0,h_1,h_2)] within 1% across all 6 admissible (h_1, h_2) tested at d=20. F4 self-consistency at h_1 = h_2 = 0: ratio = 1.00000 ± 1e-5. F5 (h_2 = 0 reduces to S205 pair × W/φ(W)): exact algebraic identity. F6 (prime triple density): 5% deviation from full HL at d=20 (standard finite-N rate). **What's new beyond S205**: S205 covers the 2-point shift case via diagonal q1=q2 contraction; S209 extends to 3-point shift where the diagonal contraction is INSUFFICIENT (off-diagonal cross-pair terms contribute essentially), but the divisor-restricted T_W^{div} factorises cleanly via S208's Möbius collapse, giving exact closed form at primorial W. **What's new beyond S208**: S208 was the 1-point identity (M_W^{div}(n) = [gcd=1]·W/φ(W)); S209 promotes this to the 3-point correlation, exhibiting the wheel-W indicator's 3-point function as exactly the prime-3-tuple HL singular series at conductor W. **What's new beyond E2.13**: E2.13 is the 4-point cube U^2 / HL match; S209 is the 3-point shift identity, a different observable in the Schwinger-Dyson hierarchy not derivable from E2.13. **What's new beyond E2.16**: E2.16 ruled out kernel-DPP/PPP descriptions of primes by noting 3-point HL factors over primes (not pairs); S209 exhibits the explicit pointwise scalar field T_W^{div} whose 3-point function REALISES this prime-factored 3-tuple singular series — turning E2.16's negative shape into a positive identity. **No polylog opening**: cost is O(N · |H|^2) per (h_1, h_2) batch on T_W^{div}; the construction provides a closed-form numerical evaluator of S_HL^{(W)} at O(W · |H|²) cost (faster than any direct prime-triple sieve), but is structural not algorithmic. **Cross-domain ingredient**: NONE — paradigm-shift session mode forbade cross-domain imports. The argument uses only Möbius inclusion-exclusion (S208), Ramanujan sums (S205), and direct prime-by-prime factorisation. **Falsifier**: any primorial W and admissible (h_1, h_2) with R_emp / pred ou